Class Abstraction and Encapsulation
- When details of implementation are encapsulated and hidden from the user


Classes and relationships
- Relationships are defined via UML
- Common relationships: association, composition, aggregation, inheritance(chap11)

Association
- Association - general, binary relationships between **two** classes
	- Adds lables, directions, roles
	- Adds multiplicity - number or interval that specifies number of objects 

	EX. Student.java(5...60) --- TAKE ---> Course.java(0...4) <--- TEACH --- Faculty.java
	
Aggregation and Composition
- Aggregation - "has a" relationship, represented with a hollow diamond, it can exist between same class: 1 to 1, or 1 to many
- Composition - depends on aggregating class, represented with a filled diamond
	- Student has a Name(composition) vs Student has an Address(aggregation)
	
Processing Primitive Data Type Values as Objects
- Primitive data types are not objects, however many java methods uses objects as arguments
- Java offers a way to incorporate a primitive data type value into an object
- Using the wrapper class , you can process primitive data type values as objects
- ex. doubleValue(), intValue(), Integer x1 = new Integer("32")

